	"nodes":[
		{"id":"61a814452442d91a","type":"text","text":"## Microeconomics\n\nThis is the branch of economics that studies how individuals and companies allocate scarce resources.\n\n(*Macroeconomics is the study of the economy as a whole*)\n\n* **Supply**: quantity of a product sellers are willing and able to supply at a given price\n* **Demand**: quantity of a product buyers are willing and able to buy at a given price\n\nMicroeconomics allows companies to determine how much to charge and how it effects competitors.\n\nAlso allows investors to analyse how profitable companies are under different scenarios.","x":-729,"y":-840,"width":540,"height":420,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"a1fce0f5c6b722d8","type":"text","text":"**Economics**: The study of production, distribution and consumption or the study of choices in the presence of scarce resources","x":-850,"y":-1059,"width":391,"height":129},
		{"id":"d937b58c68424fa3","type":"text","text":"## The Law of Demand\n\nAs the price of a product increases, consumers will buy less and vice versa.\n\n### Demand Curve\n\nThe law of demand can be represented by the demand curve. As Price increases, demand decreases.\n\n![[Pasted image 20260923170306.png]]\n\n### Shift in demand curve\n\nIf price of a substitute for pizza like sandwiches increases, leads to demand curve for pizza shifting right as more pizza demanded for each given price. (product more attractive)\n\nIf people stop liking pizza as much demand curve would shift left.\n\n![[Pasted image 20260923170538.png]]\n\n","x":-80,"y":-994,"width":800,"height":850},
		{"id":"b97f8533136de389","type":"text","text":"## Effects on Demand\n\n(things that shift the demand curve, so change quantity demanded for a fixed buy price)\n\n### Income effect\n\n* **Normal good**: if income increases, **demand increases** (like meat)\n* **Inferior good**: if income increases, **demand decreases** (like grain)\n\n### Expected future price effect\n\n* If future price increases, **demand increases**\n\n### Taste/Preference change effect\n\n* If preference improves then **demand increases** (shift right)\n\n### Substitute product price effect\n\n* Say we're looking at coke and a substitute product Pepsi.\n* Price of Pepsi **(substitute) decreasing** leads to **demand for  Coke (original) decreasing.**\n\n### Complementary product price effect\n\n* Price of complementary decreasing leads to demand for original to increase.\n* For example if price of ink decreases, demand for paper increases.\n\n### Unrelated product price effect\n\n* Demand for a product may be affected by unrelated products.\n* An example is oil prices increasing reducing demand for lots of other products like pizza.\n* Due to knock on affects and phycology, vital products increasing in price leave less money for consumers to purchase other things.","x":840,"y":-894,"width":600,"height":650},
		{"id":"416a16ebbbc1b9c9","type":"text","text":"## The Law of Supply\n\nWhen the price of a product increases, the quantity supplied increases as well.\n\n![[Pasted image 20260924122807.png]]\n\n","x":-80,"y":-80,"width":786,"height":684},
		{"id":"44f85898f91e7ace","type":"text","text":"## Effects on Supply\n\n(things that shift the supply curve, so change in quantity supplied for a fixed sell price)\n\n### Production cost effect\n\n* Technology improvements, cheaper materials/labour, lower taxes\n* If **production costs decrease**, **supply increases** for any given sell price of one unit.\n* Think about it.. so if the sell price stays the same but you sell more cars then you make more money..\n\n","x":840,"y":-80,"width":600,"height":684},
		{"id":"468fa7dddc939531","type":"text","text":"## Market Equilibrium/Equilibrium price\n\nQuantity demanded equals quantity supplied. Buyers and sellers have no incentive to change the quantity supplied or demanded at a particular price.","x":1600,"y":-289,"width":600,"height":209},
		{"id":"7d76651499251709","x":1600,"y":-1141,"width":720,"height":461,"type":"text","text":"## Elasticities of Demand\n\nElasticity: how quantity demanded or supplied changes in response to small changes in a related factor like price, income, price of substitute or related product.\n\n* Can use this to identify which companies will grow the quickest as the economy grows and vice versa.\n\n### Cross price elasticity of demand\n\n% change in quantity demanded **/** % change in price of different product\n\n### Own price elasticity of demand\n\n% change in quantity demanded **/** % change in price of product\n\n#### Example\n\n10% decrease in price of cars leads to 15% increase in quantity of cars demanded\n\n15%/-10% = -1.5\n\n* x <-1 means high elasticity, change in price means disproportionate change in demand.\n* -1< x < 0 means low elasticity, change in price means less than proportional change in demand\n* -1 just means linear relationship\n\n* **Positive price elasticity** is a **luxury good**, demand increases with price.\n* **High Negative price elasticity** means a small price cut could potentially increase demand a lot, increasing revenue.\n* **Inelastic price elasticity** means decreasing price doesn't lead to much more demand so could lose revenue. (Apple products, well defined identity)\n* **Perfectly inelastic** no change in demand with price (pharmaceuticals)\n"},
		{"id":"4a1a2bcb0274be29","x":2440,"y":-1141,"width":680,"height":461,"type":"text","text":"## Income Elasticity of Demand\n\n![[Pasted image 20260924144801.png]]"}
	],
